WEBVTT
TITLE Extendible Hashing Directory And Depths — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Static hashing fixes the bucket count first: h(k) = k mod 10, ten buckets, two records each.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Twenty-four keys into five buckets gives five, five, five, five and four records where two fit.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000
The excess goes to overflow blocks chained to the bucket, so the worst key costs four block reads instead of one.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.000
The chain grows with the file and nothing in the structure notices. A directory is the fix.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Keep an array of pointers, two to the power d of them, and index it by the low d bits of h(k).

6
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:42.000
d is the global depth: the number of low-order bits the directory reads to choose a bucket.

7
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
A lookup is one directory entry and one pointer. If the directory is resident, that is one block read, always.

8
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Two keys with different low d bits cannot share a bucket. Two keys with the same low d bits must.

9
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Each bucket also records a local depth: how many bits were actually needed to separate the records it holds.

10
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.000
A bucket at local depth two is reached by four entries of an eight-entry directory: two to the power d minus d prime.

11
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Every entry that points at one bucket agrees on the low d-prime bits. That is what makes the pointer array legal.

12
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.000
The global depth is the largest local depth, and no local depth can exceed it.

13
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Insert 22. Its binary form ends in 110, so with d equal to three the entry is 110.

14
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Entry six holds one record, 6, so 22 goes in beside it. The directory does not change.

15
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Insert 24 into entry 000, which already holds 16. Two records, capacity two: full, but not yet overflowing.

16
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The next record that lands there is the one that forces a decision, and the depths decide which decision.

17
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.000
If the local depth is below the global depth, split the bucket and leave the directory alone.

18
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Redistribute on one more bit: bit number d prime, counting from zero. A zero stays, a one moves.

19
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Half the pointers follow the new bucket. Four pointers at local depth two become two and two at local depth three.

20
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:18.000
No other bucket moves and no entry changes its index. Only the pointers that reached the split bucket are rewritten.

21
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Now the other case: the bucket is full and its local depth already equals the global depth.

22
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Only one pointer reaches it, so splitting in place would leave the new bucket with no entry pointing at it.

23
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:42.000
Doubling is a copy: the new entry at i plus two to the d is a copy of entry i.

24
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Then the split runs as before on the new bit. The global depth rises by one and the two halves take local depth d plus one.

25
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Ten records, capacity two, inserted in the order 16, 4, 6, 22, 24, 10, 31, 7, 9, 20.

26
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Three doublings and five splits take the global depth from zero to three and the directory from one entry to eight.

27
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Six buckets hold ten records, so ten of twelve slots are used. Two buckets have local depth two and are shared by two entries each.

28
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Every number here is recomputed by this page, and the four defining properties are re-checked after each of the ten inserts.

29
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Pointers reaching one bucket: two to the power d minus d prime. Depth four and local depth two gives four.

30
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Most records the structure can hold: two to the d times the capacity. Minimum depth for N records: the smallest d with two to the d at least N over the capacity.

31
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Directory size in bytes: two to the d times the pointer width. Four-byte pointers at depth twenty is four mebibytes.

32
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.000
Two to the d is an upper bound on the number of buckets, not the count. Shared entries make the count smaller.

33
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:59.000
A lookup reads one directory entry, then one bucket. One block read if the directory is resident, two if it is not.

34
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:06.000
A range query is the failure. Twenty records in twelve buckets: eight consecutive keys touch eight different buckets.

35
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:16.000
A B+ tree reads one leaf and then follows the chain. A hash index has nothing to follow.

36
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Deletion runs the split backwards. A bucket and its split image may merge if their combined records fit in one bucket.

37
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:31.000
Deleting 20 leaves a candidate of three records, so no merge. Deleting 24 leaves two, so the merge happens and the local depth falls by one.

38
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:38.000
When every local depth is below the global depth, the directory halves: each pair of entries agrees, so one of the pair is redundant.

39
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Here the halving takes the depth from three to two. Delete every record and the structure returns to one empty bucket at depth zero.

40
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Nothing here is free. Five keys all congruent to one modulo sixteen share their low four bits.

41
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Each split leaves them in the same bucket, because the bit being examined is the same for all five. The directory doubles six times.

42
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Sixty-four directory entries, seven buckets, five records. The directory grew and the records did not spread.

43
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.000
Real implementations allow a few overflow blocks and double only when overflow becomes common.

44
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:21.000
Static: one read until it overflows, then a chain, and a full rehash to grow.

45
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:28.000
Extendible: one read always, a directory that doubles, and no overflow chain except for duplicate keys.

46
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:35.000
Linear hashing: no directory at all, splits round-robin, and an overflow chain allowed in exchange.

47
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:42.000
And a B+ tree answers ranges and ordered scans, which no hash index can.

48
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:49.000
Five steps: read d, take the low d bits, follow the pointer, then choose split or double by comparing the two depths.

49
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:57.000
Write the binary of the key first and count bits from the right. Most wrong answers come from reading the wrong end.

50
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:04.000
After any split, recheck four things: array length, the two depths, pointers per bucket, and no bucket over capacity.

51
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.000
Then answer the question that was actually asked: the depth, the entry, the bucket count, or the block reads.

